rm\",\n71 |     \"power_transform\",\n72 | ]\n73 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | This file contains preprocessing tools based on polynomials.\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | import collections\n   5 | from numbers import Integral\n   6 | from itertools import chain, combinations\n   7 | from itertools import combinations_with_replacement as combinations_w_r\n   8 | \n   9 | import numpy as np\n  10 | from scipy import sparse\n  11 | from scipy.interpolate import BSpline\n  12 | from scipy.special import comb\n  13 | \n  14 | from ..base import BaseEstimator, TransformerMixin\n  15 | from ..utils import check_array\n  16 | from ..utils.validation import check_is_fitted, FLOAT_DTYPES, _check_sample_weight\n  17 | from ..utils.validation import _check_feature_names_in\n  18 | from ..utils._param_validation import Interval, StrOptions\n  19 | from ..utils.stats import _weighted_percentile\n  20 | from ..utils.fixes import sp_version, parse_version\n  21 | \n  22 | from ._csr_polynomial_expansion import (\n  23 |     _csr_polynomial_expansion,\n  24 |     _calc_expanded_nnz,\n  25 |     _calc_total_nnz,\n  26 | )\n  27 | \n  28 | \n  29 | __all__ = [\n  30 |     \"PolynomialFeatures\",\n  31 |     \"SplineTransformer\",\n  32 | ]\n```\n\nFound `examples/linear_model/plot_polynomial_interpolation.py:f`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ===================================\n  3 | Polynomial and Spline interpolation\n  4 | ===================================\n  5 | \n  6 | This example demonstrates how to approximate a function with polynomials up to\n  7 | degree ``degree`` by using ridge regression. We show two different ways given\n  8 | ``n_samples`` of 1d points ``x_i``:\n  9 | \n 10 | - :class:`~sklearn.preprocessing.PolynomialFeatures` generates all monomials\n 11 |   up to ``degree``. This gives us the so called Vandermonde matrix with\n 12 |   ``n_samples`` rows and ``degree + 1`` columns::\n 13 | \n 14 |     [[1, x_0, x_0 ** 2, x_0 ** 3, ..., x_0 ** degree],\n 15 |      [1, x_1, x_1 ** 2, x_1 ** 3, ..., x_1 ** degree],\n 16 |      ...]\n 17 | \n 18 |   Intuitively, this matrix can be interpreted as a matrix of pseudo features\n 19 |   (the points raised to some power). The matrix is akin to (but different from)\n 20 |   the matrix induced by a polynomial kernel.\n 21 | \n 22 | - :class:`~sklearn.preprocessing.SplineTransformer` generates B-spline basis\n 23 |   functions. A basis function of a B-spline is a piece-wise polynomial function\n 24 |   of degree ``degree`` that is non-zero only between ``degree+1`` consecutive\n 25 |   knots. Given ``n_knots`` number of knots, this results in matrix of\n 26 |   ``n_samples`` rows and ``n_knots + degree - 1`` columns::\n 27 | \n 28 |     [[basis_1(x_0), basis_2(x_0), ...],\n 29 |      [basis_1(x_1), basis_2(x_1), ...],\n 30 |      ...]\n 31 | \n 32 | This example shows that these two transformers are well suited to model\n 33 | non-linear effects with a linear model, using a pipeline to add non-linear\n 34 | features. Kernel methods extend this idea and can induce very high (even\n 35 | infinite) dimensional feature spaces.\n 36 | \n 37 | \"\"\"\n 38 | \n 39 | # Author: Mathieu Blondel\n 40 | #         Jake Vanderplas\n 41 | #         Christian Lorentzen\n 42 | #         Malte Londschien\n 43 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 44 | \n 45 | import numpy as np\n 46 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 47 | \n 48 | from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\n 49 | from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures, SplineTransformer\n 50 | from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n 51 | \n 52 | \n 53 | # %%\n 54 | # We start by defining a function that we intend to approximate and prepare\n 55 | # plotting it.\n 56 | \n 57 | \n 58 | def f(x):\n 59 |     \"\"\"Function to be approximated by polynomial interpolation.\"\"\"\n 60 |     return x * np.sin(x)\n 61 | \n 62 | \n 63 | # whole range we want to plot\n 64 | x_plot = np.linspace(-1, 11, 100)\n 65 | \n 66 | # %%\n 67 | # To make it interesting, we only give a small subset of points to train on.\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ================================\n  3 | Time-related feature engineering\n  4 | ================================\n  5 | \n  6 | This notebook introduces different strategies to leverage time-related features\n  7 | for a bike sharing demand regression task that is highly dependent on business\n  8 | cycles (days, weeks, months) and yearly season cycles.\n  9 | \n 10 | In the process, we introduce how to perform periodic feature engineering using\n 11 | the :class:`sklearn.preprocessing.SplineTransformer` class and its\n 12 | `extrapolation=\"periodic\"` option.\n 13 | \n 14 | \"\"\"\n 15 | \n 16 | # %%\n 17 | # Data exploration on the Bike Sharing Demand dataset\n 18 | # ---------------------------------------------------\n 19 | #\n 20 | # We start by loading the data from the OpenML repository.\n 21 | from sklearn.datasets import fetch_openml\n 22 | \n 23 | bike_sharing = fetch_openml(\n 24 |     \"Bike_Sharing_Demand\", version=2, as_frame=True, parser=\"pandas\"\n 25 | )\n 26 | df = bike_sharing.frame\n 27 | \n 28 | # %%\n 29 | # To get a quick understanding of the periodic patterns of the data, let us\n 30 | # have a look at the average demand per hour during a week.\n 31 | #\n 32 | # Note that the week starts on a Sunday, during the weekend. We can clearly\n 33 | # distinguish the commute patterns in the morning and evenings of the work days\n 34 | # and the leisure use of the bikes on the weekends with a more spread peak\n 35 | # demand around the middle of the days:\n 36 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 37 | \n 38 | \n 39 | fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 4))\n 40 | average_week_demand = df.groupby([\"weekday\", \"hour\"])[\"count\"].mean()\n 41 | average_week_demand.plot(ax=ax)\n 42 | _ = ax.set(\n 43 |     title=\"Average hourly bike demand during the week\",\n 44 |     xticks=[i * 24 for i in range(7)],\n 45 |     xticklabels=[\"Sun\", \"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\", \"Thu\", \"Fri\", \"Sat\"],\n 46 |     xlabel=\"Time of the week\",\n 47 |     ylabel=\"Number of bike rentals\",\n 48 | )\n 49 | \n 50 | # %%\n 51 | #\n 52 | # The target of the prediction problem is the absolute count of bike rentals on\n 53 | # a hourly basis:\n 54 | df[\"count\"].max()\n 55 | \n 56 | # %%\n 57 | #\n 58 | # Let us rescale the target variable (number of hourly bike rentals) to predict\n 59 | # a relative demand so that the mean absolute error is more easily interpreted\n 60 | # as a fraction of the maximum demand.\n 61 | #\n 62 | # .. note::\n 63 | #\n 64 | #     The fit method of the models used in this notebook all minimize the\n 65 | #     mean squared error to estimate the conditional mean instead of the mean\n 66 | #     absolute error that would fit an estimator of the conditional median.\n 67 | #\n 68 | #     When reporting performance measure on the test set in the discussion, we\n 69 | #     instead choose to focus on the mean absolute error that is more\n 70 | #     intuitive than the (root) mean squared error. Note, however, that the\n 71 | #     best models for one metric are also the best for the other in this\n 72 | #     study.\n 73 | y = df[\"count\"] / df[\"count\"].max()\n 74 | \n 75 | # %%\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._get_base_knot_positions`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n722 | def _get_base_knot_positions(X, n_knots=10, knots=\"uniform\", sample_weight=None):\n723 |         \"\"\"Calculate base knot positions.\n724 | \n725 |         Base knots such that first knot <= feature <= last knot. For the\n726 |         B-spline construction with scipy.interpolate.BSpline, 2*degree knots\n727 |         beyond the base interval are added.\n728 | \n729 |         Returns\n730 |         -------\n731 |         knots : ndarray of shape (n_knots, n_features), dtype=np.float64\n732 |             Knot positions (points) of base interval.\n733 |         \"\"\"\n734 |         if knots == \"quantile\":\n735 |             percentiles = 100 * np.linspace(\n736 |                 start=0, stop=1, num=n_knots, dtype=np.float64\n737 |             )\n738 | \n739 |             if sample_weight is None:\n740 |                 knots = np.percentile(X, percentiles, axis=0)\n741 |             else:\n742 |                 knots = np.array(\n743 |                     [\n744 |                         _weighted_percentile(X, sample_weight, percentile)\n745 |                         for percentile in percentiles\n746 |                     ]\n747 |                 )\n748 | \n749 |         else:\n750 |             # knots == 'uniform':\n751 |             # Note that the variable `knots` has already been validated and\n752 |             # `else` is therefore safe.\n753 |             # Disregard observations with zero weight.\n754 |             mask = slice(None, None, 1) if sample_weight is None else sample_weight > 0\n755 |             x_min = np.amin(X[mask], axis=0)\n756 |             x_max = np.amax(X[mask], axis=0)\n757 | \n758 |             knots = np.linspace(\n759 |                 start=x_min,\n760 |                 stop=x_max,\n761 |                 num=n_knots,\n762 |                 endpoint=True,\n763 |                 dtype=np.float64,\n764 |             )\n765 | \n766 |         return knots\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._more_tags`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1052 | def _more_tags(self):\n1053 |         return {\n1054 |             \"_xfail_checks\": {\n1055 |                 \"check_estimators_pickle\": (\n1056 |                     \"Current Scipy implementation of _bsplines does not\"\n1057 |           