sv`\n151 |     or `read_table` encounter non-uniform dtypes in a column(s) of a given\n152 |     CSV file.\n153 | \n154 |     See Also\n155 |     --------\n156 |     read_csv : Read CSV (comma-separated) file into a DataFrame.\n157 |     read_table : Read general delimited file into a DataFrame.\n158 | \n159 |     Notes\n160 |     -----\n161 |     This warning is issued when dealing with larger files because the dtype\n162 |     checking happens per chunk read.\n163 | \n164 |     Despite the warning, the CSV file is read with mixed types in a single\n165 |     column which will be an object type. See the examples below to better\n166 |     understand this issue.\n167 | \n168 |     Examples\n169 |     --------\n170 |     This example creates and reads a large CSV file with a column that contains\n171 |     `int` and `str`.\n172 | \n173 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n174 |     ...     {\n175 |     ...         \"a\": ([\"1\"] * 100000 + [\"X\"] * 100000 + [\"1\"] * 100000),\n176 |     ...         \"b\": [\"b\"] * 300000,\n177 |     ...     }\n178 |     ... )  # doctest: +SKIP\n179 |     >>> df.to_csv(\"test.csv\", index=False)  # doctest: +SKIP\n180 |     >>> df2 = pd.read_csv(\"test.csv\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n181 |     ... # DtypeWarning: Columns (0: a) have mixed types\n182 | \n183 |     Important to notice that ``df2`` will contain both `str` and `int` for the\n184 |     same input, '1'.\n185 | \n186 |     >>> df2.iloc[262140, 0]  # doctest: +SKIP\n187 |     '1'\n188 |     >>> type(df2.iloc[262140, 0])  # doctest: +SKIP\n189 |     <class 'str'>\n190 |     >>> df2.iloc[262150, 0]  # doctest: +SKIP\n191 |     1\n192 |     >>> type(df2.iloc[262150, 0])  # doctest: +SKIP\n193 |     <class 'int'>\n194 | \n195 |     One way to solve this issue is using the `dtype` parameter in the\n196 |     `read_csv` and `read_table` functions to explicit the conversion:\n197 | \n198 |     >>> df2 = pd.read_csv(\"test.csv\", sep=\",\", dtype={\"a\": str})  # doctest: +SKIP\n199 | \n200 |     No warning was issued.\n201 |     \"\"\"\n202 | \n203 | \n204 | class EmptyDataError(ValueError):\n205 |     \"\"\"\n206 |     Exception raised in ``pd.read_csv`` when empty data or header is encountered.\n207 | \n208 |     This error is typically encountered when attempting to read an empty file or\n209 |     an invalid file where no data or headers are present.\n210 | \n211 |     See Also\n212 |     --------\n213 |     read_csv : Read a comma-separated values (CSV) file into DataFrame.\n214 |     errors.ParserError : Exception that is raised by an error encountered in parsing\n215 |         file contents.\n216 |     errors.DtypeWarning : Warning raised when reading different dtypes in a column\n217 |         from a file.\n218 | \n219 |     Examples\n220 |     --------\n221 |     >>> from io import StringIO\n222 |     >>> empty = StringIO()\n223 |     >>> pd.read_csv(empty)\n224 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n225 |     EmptyDataError: No columns to parse from file\n226 |     \"\"\"\n227 | \n228 | \n229 | class ParserWarning(Warning):\n230 |     \"\"\"\n231 |     Warning raised when reading a file that doesn't use the default 'c' parser.\n232 | \n233 |     Raised by `pd.read_csv` and `pd.read_table` when it is necessary to change\n234 |     parsers, generally from the default 'c' parser to 'python'.\n235 | \n236 |     It happens due to a lack of support or functionality for parsing a\n237 |     particular attribute of a CSV file with the requested engine.\n238 | \n239 |     Currently, 'c' unsupported options include the following parameters:\n240 | \n241 |     1. `sep` other than a single character (e.g. regex separators)\n242 |     2. `skipfooter` higher than 0\n243 | \n244 |     The warning can be avoided by adding `engine='python'` as a parameter in\n245 |     `pd.read_csv` and `pd.read_table` methods.\n246 | \n247 |     See Also\n248 |     --------\n249 |     pd.read_csv : Read CSV (comma-separated) file into DataFrame.\n250 |     pd.read_table : Read general delimited file into DataFrame.\n251 | \n252 |     Examples\n253 |     --------\n254 |     Using a `sep` in `pd.read_csv` other than a single character:\n255 | \n256 |     >>> import io\n257 |     >>> csv = '''a;b;c\n258 |     ...           1;1,8\n259 |     ...           1;2,1'''\n260 |     >>> df = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(csv), sep=\"[;,]\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n261 |     ... # ParserWarning: Falling back to the 'python' engine...\n262 | \n263 |     Adding `engine='python'` to `pd.read_csv` removes the Warning:\n264 | \n265 |     >>> df = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(csv), sep=\"[;,]\", engine=\"python\")\n266 |     \"\"\"\n267 | \n268 | \n269 | class MergeError(ValueError):\n270 |     \"\"\"\n271 |     Exception raised when merging data.\n272 | \n273 |     Subclass of ``ValueError``.\n274 | \n275 |     See Also\n276 |     --------\n277 |     DataFrame.join : For joining DataFrames on their indexes.\n278 |     merge : For merging two DataFrames on a common set of keys.\n279 | \n280 |     Examples\n281 |     --------\n282 |     >>> left = pd.DataFrame(\n283 |     ...     {\"a\": [\"a\", \"b\", \"b\", \"d\"], \"b\": [\"cat\", \"dog\", \"weasel\", \"horse\"]},\n284 |     ...     index=range(4),\n285 |     ... )\n286 |     >>> right = pd.DataFrame(\n287 |     ...     {\"a\": [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"], \"c\": [\"meow\", \"bark\", \"chirp\", \"nay\"]},\n288 |     ...     index=range(4),\n289 |     ... ).set_index(\"a\")\n290 |     >>> left.join(\n291 |     ...     right,\n292 |     ...     on=\"a\",\n293 |     ...     validate=\"one_to_one\",\n294 |     ... )\n295 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n296 |     MergeError: Merge keys are not unique in left dataset; not a one-to-one merge\n297 |     \"\"\"\n298 | \n299 | \n300 | class AbstractMethodError(NotImplementedError):\n301 |     \"\"\"\n302 |     Raise this error instead of NotImplementedError for abstract methods.\n303 | \n304 |     The `AbstractMethodError` is designed for use in classes that follow an abstract\n305 |     base class pattern. By raising this error in the method, it ensures that a subclass\n306 |     must implement the method to provide specific functionality. This is useful in a\n307 |     framework or library where certain methods must be implemented by the user to\n308 |     ensure correct behavior.\n309 | \n310 |     Parameters\n311 |     ----------\n312 |     class_instance : object\n313 |         The instance of the class where the abstract method is being called.\n314 |     methodtype : str, default \"method\"\n315 |         A string indicating the type of method that is abstract.\n316 |         Must be one of {\"method\", \"classmethod\", \"staticmethod\", \"property\"}.\n317 | \n318 |     See Also\n319 |     --------\n320 |     api.extensions.ExtensionArray\n321 |         An example of a pandas extension mechanism that requires implementing\n322 |         specific abstract methods.\n323 |     NotImplementedError\n324 |         A built-in exception that can also be used for abstract methods but lacks\n325 |         the specificity of `AbstractMethodError` in indicating the need for subclass\n326 |         implementation.\n327 | \n328 |     Examples\n329 |     --------\n330 |     >>> class Foo:\n331 |     ...     @classmethod\n332 |     ...     def classmethod(cls):\n333 |     ...         raise pd.errors.AbstractMethodError(cls, methodtype=\"classmethod\")\n334 |     ...\n335 |     ...     def method(self):\n336 |     ...         raise pd.errors.AbstractMethodError(self)\n337 |     >>> test = Foo.classmethod()\n338 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n339 |     AbstractMethodError: This classmethod must be defined in the concrete class Foo\n340 | \n341 |     >>> test2 = Foo().method()\n342 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n343 |     AbstractMethodError: This classmethod must be defined in the concrete class Foo\n344 |     \"\"\"\n345 | \n346 |     def __init__(self, class_instance, methodtype: str = \"method\") -> None:\n347 |         types = {\"method\", \"classmethod\", \"staticmethod\", \"property\"}\n348 |         if methodtype not in types:\n349 |             raise ValueError(\n350 |                 f\"methodtype must be one of {methodtype}, got {types} instead.\"\n351 |             )\n352 |         self.methodtype = methodtype\n353 |         self.class_instance = class_instance\n354 | \n355 |     def __str__(self) -> str:\n356 |         if self.methodtype == \"classmethod\":\n357 |             name = self.class_instance.__name__\n358 |         else:\n359 |             name = type(self.class_instance).__name__\n360 |         return f\"This {self.methodtype} must be defined in the concrete class {name}\"\n361 | \n362 | \n363 | class NumbaUtilError(Exception):\n364 |     \"\"\"\n365 |     Error raised for unsupported Numba engine routines.\n366 | \n367 |     Examples\n368 |     --------\n369 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n370 |     ...     {\"key\": [\"a\", \"a\", \"b\", \"b\"], \"data\": [1, 2, 3, 4]}, columns=[\"key\", \"data\"]\n371 |     ... )\n372 |     >>> def incorrect_function(x):\n373 |     ...     return sum(x) * 2.7\n374 |     >>> df.groupby(\"key\").agg(incorrect_function, engine=\"numba\")\n375 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n376 |     NumbaUtilError: The first 2 arguments to incorrect_function\n377 |     must be ['values', 'index']\n378 |     \"\"\"\n379 | \n380 | \n381 | class DuplicateLabelError(ValueError):\n382 |     \"\"\"\n383 |     Error raised when an operation would introduce duplicate labels.\n384 | \n385 |     Examples\n386 |     --------\n387 |     >>> s = pd.Series([0, 1, 2], index=[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]).set_flags(\n388 |     ...     allows_duplicate_labels=False\n389 |     ... )\n390 |     >>> s.reindex([\"a\", \"a\", \"b\"])\n391 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n392 |        ...\n393 |     DuplicateLabelError: Index has duplicates.\n394 |           positions\n395 |     label\n396 |     a        [0, 1]\n397 |     \"\"\"\n398 | \n399 | \n400 | class InvalidIndexError(Exception):\n401 |     \"\"\"\n402 |     Exception raised when attempting to use an invalid index key.\n403 | \n404 |     Examples\n405 |     --------\n406 |     >>> idx = pd.MultiIndex.from_product([[\"x\", \"y\"], [0, 1]])\n407 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 1, 2, 2], [3, 3, 4, 4]], columns=idx)\n408 |     >>> df\n409 |         x       y\n410 |         0   1   0   1\n411 |     0   1   1   2   2\n412 |     1   3   3   4   4\n413 |     >>> df[:, 0]\n414 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n415 |     InvalidIndexError: (slice(None, None, None), 0)\n416 |     \"\"\"\n417 | \n418 | \n419 | class DataError(Exception):\n420 |     \"\"\"\n421 |     Exception raised when performing an operation on non-numerical data.\n422 | \n423 |     For example, calling ``ohlc`` on a non-numerical column or a function\n424 |     on a rolling window.\n425 | \n426 |     Examples\n427 |     --------\n428 |     >>> ser = pd.Series([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n429 |     >>> ser.rolling(2).sum()\n430 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n431 |     DataError: No numeric types to aggregate\n432 |     \"\"\"\n433 | \n434 | \n435 | class SpecificationError(Exception):\n436 |     \"\"\"\n437 |     Exception raised by ``agg`` when the functions are ill-specified.\n438 | \n439 |     The exception raised in two scenarios.\n440 | \n441 |     The first way is calling ``agg`` on a\n442 |     Dataframe or Series using a nested renamer (dict-of-dict).\n443 | \n444 |     The second way is calling ``agg`` on a Dataframe with duplicated functions\n445 |     names without assigning column name.\n446 | \n447 |     See Also\n448 |     --------\n449 |     DataFrame.agg : Aggregate using one or more operations over the specified axis.\n450 |     Series.agg : Aggregate using one or more operations over the specified axis.\n451 | \n452 |     Examples\n453 |     --------\n454 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [1, 1, 1, 2, 2], \"B\": range(5), \"C\": range(5)})\n455 |     >>> df.groupby(\"A\").B.agg({\"foo\": \"count\"})  # doctest: +SKIP\n456 |     ... # SpecificationError: nested renamer is not supported\n457 | \n458 |     >>> df.groupby(\"A\").agg({\"B\": {\"foo\": [\"sum\", \"max\"]}})  # doctest: +SKIP\n459 |     ... # SpecificationError: nested renamer is not supported\n460 | \n461 |     >>> df.groupby(\"A\").agg([\"min\", \"min\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n462 |     ... # SpecificationError: nested renamer is not supported\n463 |     \"\"\"\n464 | \n465 | \n466 | class ChainedAssignmentError(Warning):\n467 |     \"\"\"\n468 |     Warning raised when trying to set using chained assignment.\n469 | \n470 |     When the ``mode.copy_on_write`` option is enabled, chained assignment can\n471 |     never work. In such a situation, we are always setting into a temporary\n472 |     object that is the result of an indexing operation (getitem), which under\n473 |     Copy-on-Write always behaves as a copy. Thus, assigning through a chain\n474 |     can never update the original Series or DataFrame.\n475 | \n476 |     For more information on Copy-on-Write,\n477 |     see :ref:`the user guide<copy_on_write>`.\n478 | \n479 |     Examples\n480 |     --------\n481 |     >>> pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True\n482 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [1, 1, 1, 2, 2]}, columns=[\"A\"])\n483 |     >>> df[\"A\"][0:3] = 10  # doctest: +SKIP\n484 |     ... # ChainedAssignmentError: ...\n485 |     >>> pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = False\n486 |     \"\"\"\n487 | \n488 | \n489 | class NumExprClobberingError(NameError):\n490 |     \"\"\"\n491 |     Exception raised when trying to use a built-in numexpr name as a variable name.\n492 | \n493 |     ``eval`` or ``query`` will throw the error if the engine is set\n494 |     to 'numexpr'. 'numexpr' is the default engine value for these methods if the\n495 |     numexpr package is installed.\n496 | \n497 |     Examples\n498 |     --------\n499 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"abs\": [1, 1, 1]})\n500 |     >>> df.query(\"abs