ns by label(s) \\\n547 |     or a boolean array.\n548 | \n549 |     Examples\n550 |     --------\n551 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [1, 1, 1]})\n552 |     >>> df.loc[..., ..., \"A\"]  # doctest: +SKIP\n553 |     ... # IndexingError: indexer may only contain one '...' entry\n554 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [1, 1, 1]})\n555 |     >>> df.loc[1, ..., ...]  # doctest: +SKIP\n556 |     ... # IndexingError: Too many indexers\n557 |     >>> df[pd.Series([True], dtype=bool)]  # doctest: +SKIP\n558 |     ... # IndexingError: Unalignable boolean Series provided as indexer...\n559 |     >>> s = pd.Series(range(2), index=pd.MultiIndex.from_product([[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"c\"]]))\n560 |     >>> s.loc[\"a\", \"c\", \"d\"]  # doctest: +SKIP\n561 |     ... # IndexingError: Too many indexers\n562 |     \"\"\"\n563 | \n564 | \n565 | class PyperclipException(RuntimeError):\n566 |     \"\"\"\n567 |     Exception raised when clipboard functionality is unsupported.\n568 | \n569 |     Raised by ``to_clipboard()`` and ``read_clipboard()``.\n570 |     \"\"\"\n571 | \n572 | \n573 | class PyperclipWindowsException(PyperclipException):\n574 |     \"\"\"\n575 |     Exception raised when clipboard functionality is unsupported by Windows.\n576 | \n577 |     Access to the clipboard handle would be denied due to some other\n578 |     window process is accessing it.\n579 |     \"\"\"\n580 | \n581 |     def __init__(self, message: str) -> None:\n582 |         # attr only exists on Windows, so typing fails on other platforms\n583 |         message += f\" ({ctypes.WinError()})\"  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n584 |         super().__init__(message)\n585 | \n586 | \n587 | class CSSWarning(UserWarning):\n588 |     \"\"\"\n589 |     Warning is raised when converting css styling fails.\n590 | \n591 |     This can be due to the styling not having an equivalent value or because the\n592 |     styling isn't properly formatted.\n593 | \n594 |     Examples\n595 |     --------\n596 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [1, 1, 1]})\n597 |     >>> df.style.map(lambda x: \"background-color: blueGreenRed;\").to_excel(\n598 |     ...     \"styled.xlsx\"\n599 |     ... )  # doctest: +SKIP\n600 |     CSSWarning: Unhandled color format: 'blueGreenRed'\n601 |     >>> df.style.map(lambda x: \"border: 1px solid red red;\").to_excel(\n602 |     ...     \"styled.xlsx\"\n603 |     ... )  # doctest: +SKIP\n604 |     CSSWarning: Unhandled color format: 'blueGreenRed'\n605 |     \"\"\"\n606 | \n607 | \n608 | class PossibleDataLossError(Exception):\n609 |     \"\"\"\n610 |     Exception raised when trying to open a HDFStore file when already opened.\n611 | \n612 |     Examples\n613 |     --------\n614 |     >>> store = pd.HDFStore(\"my-store\", \"a\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n615 |     >>> store.open(\"w\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n616 |     \"\"\"\n617 | \n618 | \n619 | class ClosedFileError(Exception):\n620 |     \"\"\"\n621 |     Exception is raised when trying to perform an operation on a closed HDFStore file.\n622 | \n623 |     ``ClosedFileError`` is specific to operations on ``HDFStore`` objects. Once an\n624 |     HDFStore is closed, its resources are no longer available, and any further attempt\n625 |     to access data or perform file operations will raise this exception.\n626 | \n627 |     See Also\n628 |     --------\n629 |     HDFStore.close : Closes the PyTables file handle.\n630 |     HDFStore.open : Opens the file in the specified mode.\n631 |     HDFStore.is_open : Returns a boolean indicating whether the file is open.\n632 | \n633 |     Examples\n634 |     --------\n635 |     >>> store = pd.HDFStore(\"my-store\", \"a\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n636 |     >>> store.close()  # doctest: +SKIP\n637 |     >>> store.keys()  # doctest: +SKIP\n638 |     ... # ClosedFileError: my-store file is not open!\n639 |     \"\"\"\n640 | \n641 | \n642 | class IncompatibilityWarning(Warning):\n643 |     \"\"\"\n644 |     Warning raised when trying to use where criteria on an incompatible HDF5 file.\n645 |     \"\"\"\n646 | \n647 | \n648 | class AttributeConflictWarning(Warning):\n649 |     \"\"\"\n650 |     Warning raised when index attributes conflict when using HDFStore.\n651 | \n652 |     Occurs when attempting to append an index with a different\n653 |     name than the existing index on an HDFStore or attempting to append an index with a\n654 |     different frequency than the existing index on an HDFStore.\n655 | \n656 |     Examples\n657 |     --------\n658 |     >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\"], name=\"name1\")\n659 |     >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], index=idx1)\n660 |     >>> df1.to_hdf(\"file\", \"data\", \"w\", append=True)  # doctest: +SKIP\n661 |     >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"c\", \"d\"], name=\"name2\")\n662 |     >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame([[5, 6], [7, 8]], index=idx2)\n663 |     >>> df2.to_hdf(\"file\", \"data\", \"a\", append=True)  # doctest: +SKIP\n664 |     AttributeConflictWarning: the [index_name] attribute of the existing index is\n665 |     [name1] which conflicts with the new [name2]...\n666 |     \"\"\"\n667 | \n668 | \n669 | class DatabaseError(OSError):\n670 |     \"\"\"\n671 |     Error is raised when executing SQL with bad syntax or SQL that throws an error.\n672 | \n673 |     Raised by :func:`.pandas.read_sql` when a bad SQL statement is passed in.\n674 | \n675 |     See Also\n676 |     --------\n677 |     read_sql : Read SQL query or database table into a DataFrame.\n678 | \n679 |     Examples\n680 |     --------\n681 |     >>> from sqlite3 import connect\n682 |     >>> conn = connect(\":memory:\")\n683 |     >>> pd.read_sql(\"select * test\", conn)  # doctest: +SKIP\n684 |     \"\"\"\n685 | \n686 | \n687 | class PossiblePrecisionLoss(Warning):\n688 |     \"\"\"\n689 |     Warning raised by to_stata on a column with a value outside or equal to int64.\n690 | \n691 |     When the column value is outside or equal to the int64 value the column is\n692 |     converted to a float64 dtype.\n693 | \n694 |     Examples\n695 |     -----